---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { editorialPolicy } from "../lib/category-editorial";
import { pageGraphSchema } from "../lib/seo";
import { siteConfig } from "../lib/site";

const path = "/policy";
const title = `Inclusion policy | ${siteConfig.name}`;
const pageName = "Agent-first inclusion policy";
const description = "How agentfirst.directory checks Run, Equip and Enable eligibility before evidence review and classification.";
const structuredData = [pageGraphSchema({
  path, name: pageName, description, type: "WebPage",
  datePublished: "2026-10-22",
  dateModified: editorialPolicy.effectiveDate,
  lastReviewed: editorialPolicy.effectiveDate,
  breadcrumbs: [{ name: siteConfig.shortName, path: "/" }, { name: "Policy", path }],
})];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={path} structuredData={structuredData}>
  <div class="shell-inner policy-shell">
    <section class="page-intro page-intro-centered">
      <p class="detail-overline">Editorial policy {editorialPolicy.version} · Adopted {editorialPolicy.effectiveDateLabel}</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">What belongs in an agent-first directory</h1>
      <p class="page-copy">
        Tools, products, services and maintained software libraries specifically designed for agents or people
        building, running and working with agents can qualify, as can maintained agent-first protocols and standards.
        Product fit comes before evidence review. Passing fit is necessary, not acceptance.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Working definition</p>
      <h2>A concrete agent workflow, not compatibility alone</h2>
      <p>
        Identify the maintained offering and exact product mode or named standard being proposed. Someone must be
        able to install, provision, subscribe to or deliberately use it; a standard must offer a published contract
        builders can implement. Human users, upstream providers, open-source distribution and nonexclusive agent use
        do not disqualify an offering. One qualifying mode does not qualify an entire vendor family or justify a
        listing for every endpoint, skill, connector or transport.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel" id="eligibility-routes">
      <p class="detail-overline">Run · Equip · Enable</p>
      <h2>Pass through at least one eligibility route</h2>
      <ul class="policy-test-list">
        <li><strong>Run:</strong> the offering runs agents for the customer, with meaningful customer control over
          execution, state or authority. The customer can direct the operating scope and exercise a consequential
          control boundary, not merely submit a job and receive its result. Approving a finished output alone is not
          sufficient; no single named UI control is mandatory.</li>
        <li><strong>Equip:</strong> the offering supports a concrete agent task or improvement through named product
          features specifically designed for that workflow. It does not have to run agents, provision infrastructure
          or control execution, resources, state or authority. Human-operated context capture, structured feedback,
          human–agent collaboration, maintained agent-focused libraries, connector/catalogue tools and data services
          can qualify. Producing instructions or using upstream services is not an automatic exclusion.</li>
        <li><strong>Enable:</strong> a maintained protocol or interoperability standard intentionally treats agents or
          agent systems as first-class participants. Identify the agent role, concrete interaction or portable agent
          representation, system boundary and defined shared contract. Agent payments, agent-to-application events,
          identity and portable agent definitions are possible shapes. Running agents, resource ownership, exclusive
          agent use, formal recognition and multiple implementations are not prerequisites.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A runtime/control-plane shape must establish Run control; an Equip offering or Enable standard does not
        inherit that requirement. Run, Equip and Enable are eligibility routes, not classification values or category
        names. Record a primary route and any separately justified secondary route. None automatically accepts a listing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel" id="inclusion-test">
      <p class="detail-overline">Product-fit-first inclusion test</p>
      <h2>Product fit before evidence</h2>
      <p>
        Use a small positioning packet: product identity, proposed unit/mode, the intended user and core job, and
        a short agent-workflow explanation (target maximum 150 words). Use the submitted overview and, if needed,
        at most two short first-party positioning excerpts (target combined maximum 300 words), with URLs and
        section names. A relevant mode page or introductory repository/docs overview can establish positioning;
        a corporate homepage headline is not the sole authority and docs-only products are not excluded.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do not inspect claim-evidence arrays, detailed API/MCP documentation, implementation code, pricing, licensing
        or validators to make or rescue the first-gate decision. Do not browse repeatedly for a favorable phrase.
        A material contradiction or uncertain fact means hold, not cherry-picking, inferred capability or rejection
        based on omission. Page availability is separate from product fit.
      </p>
      <ul class="policy-test-list">
        <li><strong>pass-fit:</strong> record the unit/mode, route and one-sentence rationale, including Run control,
          Equip task and named features, or Enable participant and contract. Proceed to full first-party evidence,
          schema, and contradiction review. Positioning claims are not yet verified.</li>
        <li><strong>fail-fit:</strong> state the exact out-of-scope product shape and recommend nonacceptance of a new
          submission. Stop with <strong>evidence not reviewed</strong>; no evidence review, metadata enrichment or listing commits.</li>
        <li><strong>unclear-fit:</strong> state the one material gap or contradiction and request bounded clarification.
          Stop with <strong>evidence not reviewed</strong>; no evidence review, metadata enrichment or listing commits.
          No default approval or silent conversion to rejection.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Only after pass-fit</h3>
      <ol class="policy-test-list">
        <li><strong>Verify material claims.</strong> Use current first-party evidence, including contradictions and
          mode or release limits. For Enable, verify a current public specification sufficiently defines the contract
          for independent implementation. Distinguish a protocol from its harness, SDK or reference tools; shared
          branding or contradictory positioning requires clarification. Do not infer working interoperability,
          conformance or security from positioning.</li>
        <li><strong>Check listing accuracy.</strong> Verify identity, URLs, category, description, interfaces,
          deployment, claimed pricing/licensing, attribution and dates. Competition is not an exclusion.</li>
        <li><strong>Validate the exact revision.</strong> Run required content/schema/generated-data checks.
          Validation proves neither fit nor capability. Say when functional testing was not performed.</li>
        <li><strong>Record a decision.</strong> Use eligible — corrections required, hold — evidence unresolved,
          meets policy, or does not meet policy, with the remaining blockers and stage. A fit pass is not meets-policy;
          meets-policy is not permission to merge, publish or deploy.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="policy-classification-grid" id="classification-model" aria-labelledby="classifications-heading">
      <h2 id="classifications-heading" class="visually-hidden">The three classifications</h2>
      <article class="detail-panel policy-classification-card">
        <p class="detail-overline">Agent-native</p>
        <h3>Agents are core participants</h3>
        <p>The product's core abstraction, actor, runtime or participant is an agent. Removing agents would fundamentally change what the product is.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-panel policy-classification-card">
        <p class="detail-overline">Agent-enabling</p>
        <h3>Purpose-built capabilities equip agents</h3>
        <p>The offering materially enables a concrete agent workflow through agent-specific features. Infrastructure,
          libraries, connectors, data, context and human-feedback tools can fit; execution or resource ownership is not required.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-panel policy-classification-card">
        <p class="detail-overline">Agent internet protocol</p>
        <h3>Shared contracts connect agent systems</h3>
        <p>A maintained, implementable protocol or standard defines an agent interaction or portable agent representation across systems.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel policy-exclusions">
      <p class="detail-overline">What does not qualify</p>
      <h2>Agent-specific substance is required</h2>
      <ul class="policy-test-list">
        <li>Generic products with only API/MCP compatibility, an AI label or a repackaged interface.</li>
        <li>Standalone prompt packs, skill files, templates and one-off scripts. Maintained software is not a prompt pack merely because it generates instructions.</li>
        <li>Individual endpoints or adapters without an identifiable maintained offering that independently qualifies.</li>
        <li>Finished business-outcome apps without a qualifying customer-operated agent or agent-service boundary. Internal AI alone does not qualify.</li>
        <li>Generic protocols with only incidental agent use, or products claiming to be standards solely because they implement MCP, x402 or another protocol.</li>
        <li>Unsupported marketing claims. A dashboard, token vault, retries, tool count or a new landing page alone does not establish agent-specific design.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For a coding-agent orchestrator claiming Run, the system must own a material lifecycle across runs: task
        assignment, isolation, retained or shared state or validation evidence, and a completion, review or landing
        gate separate from execution. Merely launching one agent is insufficient for that claim. This Run-specific
        boundary is not a universal requirement for Equip or Enable; category guidance cannot override the inclusion routes.
      </p>
      <p>
        After successful evidence review, choose exactly one supported classification: agent-native, agent-enabling
        or agent-internet-protocol. Category describes the capability area; classification explains inclusion.
        Neither is a quality score, security endorsement or substitute for due diligence.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="detail-panel policy-panel">
      <p class="detail-overline">Accountability</p>
      <h2>How this policy is applied</h2>
      <p>
        The adopted v0.3 source is <a class="detail-link" href={editorialPolicy.sourceUrl}>policy issue #125</a>.
        The coordinated repository counterpart is <a class="detail-link"
          href="https://github.com/bradvin/agentfirst.directory/pull/145/files">docs/editorial-policy.md in source PR #145</a>
        (available after the companion source-policy PR merges; until then, use issue #125).
        This policy takes precedence over conflicting category or contribution guidance. Record policy version,
        source revision/access date, unit/mode, workflow, route and rationale, positioning sources, fit decision,
        availability gaps, stage, evidence consulted (or evidence not reviewed), actual validation, unresolved facts
        and recommendation with its approval boundary. Owner-associated services and competitors receive the same test.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the <a class="detail-link" href="/editorial-standards">editorial standards</a> for research and review,
        or the <a class="detail-link" href="/corrections">corrections and appeals process</a> to challenge a claim.
        Historical reassessments and public corrections require separate owner approval; this update authorizes no
        existing-listing changes. It does not activate the installed reviewer or restart paused submission review or publishing.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="policy-cta">
      <p>Have a tool with a concrete agent workflow?</p>
      <a class="button button-primary" href="/submit">Read the submission guide</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
